{"version":"1472","text":"arXiv:2505.08982v1 [cs.LG] 13 May 2025\nModel-free Online Learning for the Kalman Filter: Forgetting Factor and Logarithmic Regret ∗\nJiachen Qian1 and Yang Zheng1\n1Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of California San Diego\nMay 15, 2025\nAbstract\nWe consider the problem of online prediction for an unknown, non-explosive linear stochastic system. With a known system model, the optimal predictor is the celebrated Kalman filter. In the case of unknown systems, existing approaches based on recursive least squares and its variants may suffer from degraded performance due to the highly imbalanced nature of the regression model. This imbalance can easily lead to overfitting and thus degrade prediction accuracy. We tackle this problem by injecting an inductive bias into the regression model via exponential forgetting. While exponential forgetting is a common wisdom in online learning, it is typically used for re-weighting data. In contrast, our approach focuses on balancing the regression model. This achieves a better trade-off between regression and regularization errors, and simultaneously reduces the accumulation error. With new proof techniques, we also provide\na sharper logarithmic regret bound of O(log3 N ), where N is the number of observations.\n1 Introduction\nThe problem of sequential online prediction in linear dynamical systems is fundamental across many fields and has a long-standing history [1]. Its applications span from control systems [2], robotics [3], natural language processing [4], and computer vision [5]. The celebrated Kalman filter [1] predicts the future system state and observation by properly filtering the past observations. When the underlying dynamics are linear with Gaussian noise, the Kalman filter achieves optimal prediction among all possible filters by minimizing the mean square error. Many structural properties of the Kalman filter, including stability and statistical properties, have been well-established [2]. Extensions to nonlinear systems have also been well developed in the past [6, 7]. All these classical developments on the Kalman filter and its variants rely on exact knowledge of the system model and noise statistics. However, when the system model and/or noise statistics are unknown, learning-based approaches are required to predict future observations. In this paper, we consider the problem of predicting observations in an unknown, partially observed, nonexplosive linear dynamical system driven by Gaussian noise using finite past observations. Learning for prediction is not a new topic and has a rich list of literature; see [8, 9] for classical textbooks. This topic has recently received renewed interest from the online learning perspective [10–12].\n∗This work is supported by NSF ECCS 2154650, NSF CMMI 2320697, and NSF CAREER 2340713. Emails: jiq012@ucsd.edu; zhengy@ucsd.edu.\n1\n\n\n Table 1: Regrets in Recent Online Prediction Algorithms\nAlgorithms Regret Gaussian Forgetting\nHazan et al., 2017 [10] O ̃(√N ) × ×\nKozdoba et al., 2019 [19] O ̃(√N + ǫN ) × ×\nLi et al., 2024 [21] O ̃(√N ) × ×\nRashidinejad et al., 2020 [20] O(log11 N ) √ ×\nTsiamis and Pappa, 2023 [12] O(log6 N ) √ ×\nOur paper O(log3 N ) √ √\nIn this table, “Gaussian” means whether the system noises are assumed to be Gaussian; “Forgetting” means whether any forgetting strategy is used.\nThe methodology of learning for prediction can be broadly divided into two categories: the indirect method and the direct method. The indirect method first exploits the past data to build an approximation of the nominal system [13–15] and then designs a filter for prediction based on the identified model. However, learning directly the model parameters often leads to a nonlinear and nonconvex problem even for linear dynamical systems [16]. Many adaptive filtering techniques have been developed to address unknown models or changing environments [9, 17]. These developments are typically based on extended least squares and variants for parameter estimation and state prediction, and the classical analysis is often based on asymptotic tools assuming infinite samples. The direct method aims to design a filter by directly learning the optimal prediction policy from data. This perspective has gained increasing attention in the online learning framework [10, 1820]. In this context, the goal of model estimation is replaced by regret minimization in online prediction, which measures the loss compared to the best prediction in hindsight. For example, a low-rank approximation technique was proposed in [10], and [19] utilized structural properties of the Kalman filter to derive a regression model where the output is the optimal prediction and the input is previous observations. The regret guarantees in [10, 19] are sublinear. A recent study [21] also presents an online learning strategy to achieve a sublinear regret. The first algorithmic regret was established in [12], where a truncated regression model was used. Another study [20] also established a logarithmic regret for a low-rank spectral approximation strategy; see Table 1 for a comparison. One main challenge in online prediction is handling non-explosive systems, where the system state exhibits a long-term memory effect and may even grow at a polynomial rate. This long-term memory effect can introduce a persistent bias in the regression model [19]. To mitigate the bias, it is crucial to gradually expand the past data horizon for prediction [12]. However, due to the recursive nature of the Kalman filter, the resulting regression model becomes highly imbalanced, with each block potentially differing by several orders of magnitude. This imbalance can easily lead to overfitting (see Figure 1), thus degrading the regret performance of the existing online prediction algorithms [10, 12, 19, 20]. Forgetting strategies, also known as discounting, are widely used to mitigate overfitting and adapt to non-stationary environments [22–24]. These techniques prioritize recent information over outdated data, ensuring models remain responsive to changing dynamics. A common approach is exponential forgetting, where past observations are down-weighted using a forgetting factor, preventing the dominance of earlier data in recursive updates. This technique is frequently employed in recursive least squares and adaptive Kalman filtering [9, 25–27]. However, most existing forgetting strategies focus on re-weighting past and recent data, which does not effectively address the imbalanced structure inherent in online learning of the Kalman filter.\n2\n\n\n Figure 1: Illustration of the overfitting effect in a simple example. The black curve shows the ground truth that has a highly imbalanced structure, and the blue curve is the estimated model using method provided in [12] that overfits the small blocks.\nIn this paper, we address the imbalanced regression model in model-free online learning of the Kalman filter. We also aim to derive a sharper logarithmic regret bound of our online prediction algorithm. Our technical contributions are twofold.\n• At the algorithmic level, we introduce an Online Prediction with Forgetting (OPF) algorithm which uses an exponentially forgetting strategy to properly re-balance the regression model. Our forgetting approach aims to directly balance the regression model, whereas most existing discounting and forgetting methods primarily aim to re-weight the online data [24, 27]. We further show that our forgetting strategy is equivalent to a generalized Ridge regression, which introduces an inductive bias reflecting the exponentially decaying structure in the regression model. Our OPF algorithm effectively mitigates the overfitting effect and demonstrates improved performance compared to existing online prediction algorithms [10, 12, 20].\n• At the level of theoretical guarantees, the best theoretical regret bound previously established is O(log6 N ) for marginally stable system [12], where N is the number of observations. In this paper, we establish a much shaper regret bound of O(log3 N ) by new analysis techniques, including Hanson-Wright inequality based martingale analysis and self-cancellation property of accumulation error. Table 1 lists a comparison of the bounds in recent online prediction algorithms. Note that our use of the forgetting factor may not directly reduce the order of the logarithmic regret bound. Instead, it effectively controls accumulation error and achieves a better trade-off between regression error and regularization error. This significantly improves the constant factors in the regret bound, as demonstrated in our numerical experiments.\nThe rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents preliminaries on the Kalman filter. We introduce the new OPF algorithm in Section 3, and its regret guarantee is established in Section 4. Numerical experiments are presented in Section 5. Section 6 concludes this paper. Further discussions and proof details are presented in the appendix.\n2 Preliminaries and Problem Formulation\n2.1 Background on the Kalman Filter\nConsider a partially observed linear stochastic system\nxk+1 = Axk + wk, wk ∼ N (0, Q)\nyk = Cxk + vk, vk ∼ N (0, R), (1)\n3\n\n\n where xk ∈ Rn is the state vector, and yk ∈ Rm is the output measurement, A ∈ Rn×n is the state transition matrix, and C ∈ Rm×n is the observation matrix. The time series wk and vk denote the process and measurement noises, respectively. We assume they are zero-mean independent identically distributed (i.i.d) Gaussian variables with covariances Q ≻ 0 and R ≻ 0 respectively. An important problem is to predict the observation yt+1 based on past measurements, a process commonly referred to as filtering or prediction1. To guarantee the existence of a stable filter, we make a standard assumption.\nAssumption 1. The system pair (A, C) is detectable.\nLet Fk , σ (y0, . . . , yk) be the filtration generated by the observations y0, . . . , yk. Given past observations up to time k, we consider the problem of optimal prediction yˆk+1 at time k + 1 in the minimum mean-square error sense:\nyˆk+1 , arg min\nz∈Fk\nE\n[\n‖yk+1 − z‖2\n2 | Fk\n]\n. (2)\nThanks to the foundational work [1], it is now well-known that the optimal predictor takes a recursive form, known as the Kalman filter,\nxˆk+1 = Axˆk + Lk (yk − yˆk) , xˆ0 = 0\nyˆk = Cxˆk, (3)\nwhere Lk = APkCT (CPkCT + R)−1 with Pk satisfying\nPk+1 = APkAT + Q − APkCT (\nCPkCT + R\n)−1 CPkAT. (4)\nWith Assumption 1, Pk in the recursion (4) converges to a steady-state P exponentially fast, which leads to the well-studied algebraic Riccati equation below [28]\nP = AP AT + Q − AP CT (\nCPCT + R\n)−1 CP AT. (5)\nThe steady-state Kalman gain is denoted as L. We remark that the error ek+1 = yk+1 − yˆk+1 is often referred to as the innovation noise process, which represents the amount of new information yk+1 brings beyond what is already known from previous observations up to yk.\n2.2 Model-free Prediction and Regret\nThe Kalman filter in (3) requires the system matrices A and C as well as noise statistics Q and R. This is thus a model-based prediction, with its performance heavily dependent on the availability and accuracy of the system model. In this paper, we focus on model-free online learning algorithms to predict observations based only on past measurements, without requiring any knowledge of the system matrices or noise covariances. Following [12, 20], we quantify the performance of the online prediction in terms of the regret measured against the Kalman filter (3) that has full system knowledge. The goal is\n1Depending on the context, filtering and prediction may have slightly different meanings [2]; we here treat them as equivalent concepts. Also, all our discussions can be applied to linear systems with inputs; see Appendix F.1.\n4\n\n\n to design an online algorithm y ̃k = fk(y0, . . . , yk−1) such that with probability at least 1 − δ, the following regret\nRN ,\nN ∑\nk=1\n‖yk − y ̃k‖2 −\nN ∑\nk=1\n‖yk − yˆk‖2 , (6)\nwhere yˆk is the steady-state Kalman filter’s prediction, is bounded by poly(log 1/δ)o(N ). Note that, due to the exponential convergence of the Kalman filter to its steady-state, it is sufficient to compare the online algorithm with the steady-state predictor yˆk. The regret benchmark in (6) is much stronger than that in [10]. Still, recent advances in [12, 20] have shown that it is possible to obtain logarithmic regrets.\nOne key observation is that the Kalman filter (3) has a linear regression structure after expanding the past p observations:\nyk = GpZk,p + C(A − LC)pxˆk−p + ek, (7)\nwhere ek = yk −yˆk, and Zk,p denotes the past p observations at time k, i.e., Zk,p , [ykT−p . . . ykT−1\n]T ,\nand Gp denotes the Markov parameters of the closed-loop filter:\nGp , [C(A − LC)p−1L, · · · , CL] ∈ Rm×pm. (8)\nTo simplify discussions, we make another assumption.\nAssumption 2. ([12, 20]) The matrix A − LC is diagonalizable.\nWhile (7) is straightforward to derive based on the structure of Kalman filter (3), this linear regression model provides sufficient insights: the system output at time k can be viewed as a linear combination of the past observations with an additional bias term C(A − LC)pxˆk−p and the innovation noise process ek. Under Assumption 1, it is well known that 1) the spectral radius satisfies ρ(A − LC) < 1, and 2) ek are mutually uncorrelated Gaussian random variables with zero-mean [2]. Thus, the model-free prediction can be reduced to an online least-squares problem (7). Similar ideas can be traced back to subspace identifications [29].\n2.3 Long-term Memory and Forgetting\nWith (7), it is clear that the problem of model-free prediction for system (1) falls within the framework of online least squares [30]. Two recent works [11, 12] have used basic recursive auto-regression algorithms to estimate Gp and make online predictions. To reduce the dimension of Gp, [20] has embedded spectral approximation into algorithm design based on the concept of Kolmogorov width. However, the practical performance of these existing approaches can easily deteriorate due to the highly imbalanced nature of the regression model (7). Indeed, for nonexplosive systems ρ(A) ≤ 1, the state xˆk−p has a long-term memory effect and leads to a persistent bias term. A suitable past horizon length p is required to mitigate xˆk−p in (7). Since ρ(A − LC) < 1, the magnitude of each\nblock in Gp exponentially decays to 0, i.e., ∥\n∥C(A − LC)tL∥\n∥2 ≤ M ρ(A − LC)t under Assumption 2,\nwhere M is a constant. This imbalance can easily lead to overfitting during online learning, thereby degrading the prediction accuracy of methods in [11, 12, 19, 20]. Figure 1 illustrates this overfitting effect on a simple example. The magnitude of the true regression model Gp (represented by the\nblack line) is significantly smaller than the estimates G ̃p from the algorithm in [12]. The basic least-squares algorithms in [11, 12, 20] can easily overfit the small values of blocks C(A − LC)tL.\n5\n\n\n In this paper, our key idea is to use an exponentially forgetting strategy to properly re-balance the regression model and mitigate overfitting, thus improving the online prediction performance. As we will show in Section 3, our forgetting approach aims to directly balance the regression model itself, whereas most existing discounting and forgetting methods primarily aim to re-weight the online data, as seen in the recent works [24, 27]. We further show that our forgetting strategy is equivalent to a generalized Ridge regression, which introduces an inductive bias reflecting the exponentially decaying structure (8) in the regression (7).\n3 Online Prediction with Forgetting\nThis section introduces a new Online Prediction with Forgetting (OPF) algorithm. We also show that our forgetting strategy is equivalent to a generalized ridge regression with an inductive bias to re-balance the regression model.\n3.1 Online Balanced Regression via Forgetting\nOur approach begins by addressing the exponentially decaying structure of the regressor Gp in (8). The key idea is to appropriately rescale the blocks as follows\nG ̃p ,\n[1\nγp−1 C(A − LC)p−1L, · · · , 1\nγ C(A − LC)L, CL\n]\n,\nwhere γ ∈ (0, 1). If γ is close to ρ(A − LC), then the magnitude of each bock 1\nγt C(A − LC)tL will\nremain relatively balanced across all terms. With this idea, we rewrite the regression model (7) as\nyk = G ̃pZ ̃k,p\n} {{ }\nBalanced regression\n+ C(A − LC)pxˆk−p\n} {{ } Long-term bias\n+ }e{k{}\nInnovation noise\n, (9)\nwhere we have G ̃p = GpDp−1 and Z ̃k,p := DpZk,p with\nDp = diag(γp−1, γp−2, . . . , γ, 1) ⊗ Im (10)\nbeing a re-weighting matrix. The new regression model (9) does not change the long-term bias term C(A − LC)pxˆk−p or the innovation noise term ek. It is also worthwhile noting that the balanced regression (9) aims to express the output at time k as a linear combination of the past observations with exponentially forgetting. In other words, we have\nZ ̃k,p = DpZk,p =\n\n   \nγp−1yk−p γp−2yk−p+1\n...\nyk−1\n\n   \n. (11)\nInstead of optimizing over the original Markov parameters Gp in (8) which easily leads to overfitting,\nwe optimize over the re-balanced G ̃p in (9). Note that optimizing over G ̃p is known as improper learning in the context of online learning [31], as we do not directly learn the true model parameters\nin the hypothesis class H = (A, C, Q, R) but re-parameterize and learn over a different class H ̃.\n6\n\n\n We learn a least-squares estimate G ̃k,p by regressing yt to past outputs Z ̃t,p, t ≤ k with exponentially forgetting, i.e.,\nG ̃k,p =\nk ∑\nt=p\nytZ ̃tT,p\n(\nλI +\nk ∑\nt=p\nZ ̃t,pZ ̃tT,p\n)−1\n, (12)\nwhere λ > 0 is a regularization parameter and the matrix\nV ̃k,p , λI +\nk ∑\nt=p\nZ ̃t,pZ ̃tT,p (13)\nis called a Gram matrix, which contains the collected information of all the past knowledge Z ̃k,p. We then predict the next observation by computing\ny ̃k+1 = G ̃k,pZ ̃k+1,p. (14)\nFor nonexplosive systems ρ(A) ≤ 1, the state xˆk−p retains a long-term memory effect, and its value may even grow at a polynomial rate. A persistent bias error could result in linear regret. Fortunately, classical Kalman filter theory provides two key insights: 1) the innovation noise ek is zero mean and mutually uncorrelated, and 2) ρ(A−LC) < 1. The fact that the spectral radius is less than one enables us to manage the accumulation of long-term bias errors effectively. Specifically, we can gradually increase the past horizon p, an approach aligned with the strategy in [12] and also motivated by a common “doubling trick” [32]. In particular, the entire time horizon is partitioned into multiple epochs, with each successive epoch being twice as long as the previous one. Within each epoch, the value of p past horizon remains constant. Given that ρ(A − LC)p is decreasing exponentially, it is sufficient to slowly increase the past horizon length as p = O(log T ), where T is the length of each epoch. The pseudocode of our online prediction with forgetting (OPF) is listed in Algorithm 1, which has two main phases: Warm-Up and Online Prediction. In the warm-up phase, we collect a trajectory of observations of length Tinit. In the phase of online prediction, we first initialize the parameter\np, Dp, V ̃Tl−1,p and G ̃Tl−1,p at the beginning of each epoch Tl. At each time step k, we update the prediction y ̃k using (14) and then observe the new observation yk. Within each epoch, the predictor can be computed recursively\nV ̃k,p = V ̃k−1,p + DpZk,pZkT,pDp, (15a)\nG ̃k,p = G ̃k−1,p + (yk − y ̃k) ZkT,pDpV ̃ −1\nk,p . (15b)\nAlgorithm 1 requires no knowledge of the system model or noise covariances. Even the state dimension n is not needed as we only predict the output. Note that the past horizon p = β log Tl deals with the long-term memory x ̃k−p in (9). Selecting a larger β leads to a smaller bias error but\nincreases the estimation variance of G ̃p due to larger freedom.\nRemark 1 (Forgetting strategies). The idea of forgetting factor is widely used in online learning [24, 33–35]. To our knowledge, most existing studies only focus on balancing current and past data by applying a horizon-dependent forgetting factor uniformly to each sample αk−tZt,p. In our context, this leads to an updating law below:\nG ̃k,p =\nk ∑\nt=p\nαk−tytZtT,p\n(\nλI +\nk ∑\nt=p\nαk−tZt,pZtT,p\n)−1\n. (16)\n7\n\n\n Algorithm 1 Online Prediction with Forgetting (OPF)\n1: Input: parameter β, λ, γ, Tinit , NE {Warm Up:}\n2: for k = 0 to Tinit do 3: Observe yk; 4: end for\n{Recursive Online Prediction:} 5: for l = 1 to NE do\n6: Initialize Tl = 2l−1Tinit + 1, p = β log Tl, Dp in (10)\n7: Re-balance Z ̃t,p = DpZk,p in (11) for p ≤ t < Tl;\n8: Compute V ̃Tl−1, p in (13) and G ̃Tl−1,p in (12); 9: for k = Tl to 2Tl − 2 do\n10: Predict y ̃k = G ̃k−1,pZ ̃k,p; 11: Observe yk;\n12: Update V ̃k,p and G ̃k,p recursively as (15). 13: end for 14: end for\nHere, α ∈ (0, 1) is used to distinguish it from γ. However, (16) can result in information loss due to exponential forgetting, which, in turn, increases prediction errors, particularly in marginally stable systems [35, Lemma D.5]. In contrast, our OPF method leverages the forgetting factor to\nprovide a non-uniform scaling for each sample Z ̃k,p = DpZk,p. This approach effectively balances the regression model with no information loss, thereby reducing the overfitting effect. Both theoretical guarantees in Section 4 and numerical experiments in Section 5 confirm our intuition.\n3.2 Inductive Bias from Forgetting\nWe here show that the exponentially forgetting strategy in our OPF algorithm is equivalent to solving a generalized ridge regression with appropriate regularization. This regularization introduces an inductive bias that captures our prior belief about the underlying imbalanced structure, such that our OPF algorithm never forgets.\nRecall the re-scaled data samples at time step k in (11), i.e., Z ̃k,p = DpZk,p. We define the two matrices as\nV ̃k,p , λI +\nk ∑\nt=p\nZ ̃t,pZ ̃tT,p, V ̄k,p , λDp−2 +\nk ∑\nt=p\nZt,pZtT,p.\nIt is clear that both are positive definite. The modified cross-term is defined as S ̃k,p , ∑k\nl=p ylZ ̃lT,p =\nSk,pDp. Then, the update of the regression coefficient in (12) becomes G ̃k,p = S ̃k,pV ̃ −1\nk,p and the prediction of OPF at time step k + 1 is given in (14). We now have the following equivalence\nS ̃k,pV ̃ −1\nk,p Z ̃k+1,p =Sk,p\n(\nλDp−2 +\nk ∑\nk=1\nZk,pZkT,p\n)−1\nZk+1,p\n=Sk,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Zk+1,p. (17)\n8\n\n\n Thus, applying our exponentially forgetting factor γ is mathematically equivalent to replacing the\nregularization term λI with λDp−2. More specifically, G ̃k,p in (12) is the solution to the following generalized ridge regression\nmin\nG∈Rm×mp\nk ∑\nt=p\n‖yt − GZt,p‖2\nF +∥\n∥λGDp−1\n∥\n∥2\nF . (18)\nThe original ridge regression for (7) is similar to (18) but with a usual regularization ‖λG‖2\nF . For\n(18), we can see that the forgetting factor γ acts as a regularization ∥\n∥λGDp−1\n∥\n∥2\nF . Indeed, this\nregularization injects our prior belief that the ground truth of G has an exponentially decaying structure as in (8), with the penalty term 1\nγt effectively reducing the effect of overfitting.\n4 Logarithmic Regret of the OPF Algorithm\nWe here prove that with high probability, the regret of our OPF in Algorithm 1 is logarithmic of the order log3 N , which greatly improves the existing results (see Table 1).\n4.1 A Sharper Logarithmic Regret\nRecall that the regret RN in (6) measures against the optimal Kalman filter’s prediction with true model knowledge. We have the following logarithmic guarantee for our proposed OPF in Algorithm 1.\nTheorem 1. Consider the linear stochastic system (1) with Q ≻ 0, R ≻ 0, and suppose Assumptions 1 and 2 hold. Choose the forgetting factor γ ∈ (ρ(A − LC), 1], and the parameters in Algorithm 1 as\nβ = Ω(κ)\nlog(1/ρ(A − LC)) , Tinit = poly\n(\nβ, log\n(1\nδ\n))\n,\nwhere κ represents the order of the largest Jordan block of A corresponding to the eigenvalue 1. Fix a horizon N > Tinit and a failure probability δ > 0. Then with probability at least 1 − δ, we have\nRN ≤ poly (M, β, log(1/δ)) O (log3 N ) ,\nwhere M is a constant only related to the system parameter.\nAchieving a logarithmic regret is not surprising since we learn the regression model (9), which is in the framework of online least-squares [30]. Given the fact that (A−LC)p is exponentially decaying and the innovation noise ek is mutually uncorrelated Gaussian, selecting a large p = O(log Tl) in each epoch is sufficient to obtain an online predictor y ̃k with logarithmic regret. In [10, 21], the system noise is not guaranteed to be i.i.d. Gaussian, hence the algorithms therein do not have logarithmic regrets. In two recent studies [12, 20] which focused on the original unbalanced regression model\n(7), the regret bound is shown to be O (log6 N ) and O (log11 N ), respectively. In Theorem 1, we have established a much shaper bound compared with the existing results; also see Table 1 for a comparison. Our proof techniques involve new tools from Hanson-Wright inequality-based martingale analysis and self-cancellation property of accumulation error. Our Theorem 1 confirms that our idea of forgetting factor effectively balances the regression model with no information loss. Note that the forgetting factor γ may not directly reduce the\n9\n\n\n order of the regret to log(N ), but it can effectively reduce the accumulation error, and balance a better trade-off between regression error and regularization error, thus significantly reducing some constant in the logarithmic regret. In Appendix D, we provide further discussions on the effect of the forgetting factor.\n4.2 Proof Sketches for the Shaper Regret\nIn this subsection, we provide proof sketches for Theorem 1. Rather than presenting all proof details (which are given in Appendix C), we here highlight the main theoretical tools that achieve a sharper regret bound compared with the state-of-the-art in Table 1.\nStep 1: Accumulation error and martingale terms. From general online linear regression techniques [12, 20], we first divide the regret RN into two parts, i.e.,\nRN ,\nN ∑\nk=Tinit\n‖yk − y ̃k‖2\n2−\nN ∑\nk=Tinit\n‖yk − yˆk‖2\n2\n=\nN ∑\nk=Tinit\n‖yˆk − y ̃k‖2\n2\n} {{ } LN\n+2\nN ∑\nk=Tinit\nekT (yˆk − y ̃k)\n} {{ } martingale term\n. (19)\nThe first part is the accumulation of the gap between yˆk − y ̃k, while the second part is a cross term between the innovation ek and the gap yˆk − y ̃k. Since ek is i.i.d. Gaussian, from the self-normalized martingale theory [13, 36], it is standard to bound\nN ∑\nk=Tinit\nekT (yˆk − y ̃k) = O ̃\n(√LN\n)\n= o (LN ) , (20)\ni.e., the cross term is dominated by the accumulation term LN . In the rest of the proof, we will tightly bound LN .\nStep 2: Regularization, regression and bias errors. Following standard linear regression techniques [10, 36, 37], we can divide the gap yˆk − y ̃k into three parts:\n• 1) the regularization error induced by λDp−2,\n• 2) the regression error induced by Gaussian error ek, and\n• 3) the bias error induced by the long-term memory bk,p = C(A − KC)pxˆk−p.\nConsequently, we rewrite the gap y ̃k − yˆk at each time as\ny ̃k+1 − yˆk+1 =\nk ∑\nl=p\nbl,pZlT,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Zk+1,p − bk+1,p\n} {{ } Bias error\n+\nk ∑\nl=p\nelZlT,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Zk+1,p\n} {{ } Regression error\n− λGpDp−2V ̄ −1\nk,p Zk+1,p } {{ } Regularization error\n. (21)\nOne main difference between (21) with that in [12, 20] is the scaling matrix Dp induced by the forgetting factor γ (see (10)) will simultaneously affect the regularization error and the inverse\n10\n\n\n of the Gram matrix V ̄ −1\nk,p . We will provide a detailed discussion on the effect of Dp for a better trade-off between regularization and accumulation in Appendix D. From (21), we can bound LN by\nLN =\nN ∑\nk=Tinit\n‖y ̃k+1 − yˆk+1‖2\n2\n≤6 sup\nTinit≤k≤N\n(∥ ∥ 