r bound on the regret LN from O(log6 N ) [12] to O(log3 N ). When implementing Algorithm 1, the parameter p should be an integer. Thus, we need to use p = ⌈β log Tl⌉, but rounding up to an integer will not affect the order of regret.\n5 Numerical Experiments\nIn this section, we compare our proposed OPF in Algorithm 1 with the algorithm in [12] and traditional forgetting techniques [24]. Further experiments are given in Appendix E. We first consider an augmented form of the dynamical system model from [12, Section V], where the system parameters are defined as\nA = I3 ⊗ A ̃, C = I3 ⊗ C ̃, Q =\n\n\nI3 0.2I3 0.2I3 0.2I3 I3 0.2I3 0.2I3 0.2I3 I3\n\n\nwhere\nA ̃ =\n\n\n11 0 01 1 0 0 0.9\n\n , C ̃ = [1 0 0] , R = I3.\n12\n\n\n (a) Our Forget\n6500 6600 6700 6800 6900 7000\n1.15\n1.2\n1.25\n1.3\n1.35\n1.4 104\n(b) Traditional Forget\nFigure 2: Performance comparison of online prediction with different forgetting strategies: (a) our proposed forgetting; (b) traditional forgetting in [24].\nThis can be interpreted as a 3-D target tracking problem with an unknown correlated dynamic model [40]. The parameters in our OPF algorithm are chosen as\nβ = 2.5, Tinit = 60, λ = 1, NE = 7.\nAccordingly, the total horizon is 7680. The comparison result with different forgetting factor γ for Algorithm 1 is shown in Figure 2(a), where γ = 1 represents the performance of the algorithm in [12]. From Figure 2(a), we can see that the performance of γ = ρ(A − LC) performs better than other forgetting factor γ ∈ (ρ(A − LC), 1]. We also implemented the traditional forgetting techniques (16) from [24] for online prediction of marginally stable systems. As we can see from Figure 2(b), only for α = 0.9999, almost close to 1, the performance of the online prediction algorithm is slightly improved. When α is chosen to be 0.99, the forgetting-based method performs poorly compared with the non-forgetting case α = 1, which verifies our discussions in Remark 1. Hence, the forgetting technique proposed in this paper is a more effective way to improve the performance of online prediction. This is incorporated in our OPF algorithm. In our second numerical experiment, we verify that the forgetting factor γ can effectively reduce the regret RN . In Appendix D, we prove that the forgetting factor can reduce the accumulation\nerror ∑N\nk=Tinit\n∥\n∥V ̄ − 1\n2\nk,p Zk+1,p\n∥\n∥ and regression error ∥\n∥Ek,pZ ̄kT,pV ̄ − 1\n2\nk,p\n∥\n∥2\n2, while not increasing the regu\nlarization error ∥\n∥λGpDp−2V ̄ − 1\n2\nk,p\n∥\n∥2\n2 too much. In Figure 3(a), we choose the accumulation error at the\nend of each epoch for different γ as the performance index. In Figure 3(b), we choose the regression error and regularization error for different γ at time step N for comparison, where the shaded area is calculated with one standard deviation. The result in Figure 3 is consistent with our claim in Appendix D.\n6 Conclusion\nIn this paper, we addressed the problem of online prediction for an unknown, non-explosive linear stochastic system. To counteract the imbalanced regression model, which degrades the performance of existing prediction algorithms, we introduced an exponential forgetting strategy in our OPF\n13\n\n\n (a) Accumulation Error (b) Trade-Off\nFigure 3: Effect of our forgetting factor on different parts of regret RN in (22).\nalgorithm that injects an inductive bias. This approach leads to a more balanced regression model and helps mitigate overfitting. Using refined proof techniques, we developed a structured regret analysis framework, particularly for marginally stable systems, and we established a sharper regret bound as O(log3 N ). Our findings highlight the importance of balancing the regression model rather than just re-weighting past data. Future directions include exploring adaptive forgetting strategies and extending the framework to nonlinear systems, such as special classes of structured nonlinear models [41].\nReferences\n[1] R. E. Kalman. A New Approach to 