e forgetting factor γ influences the regret RN by affecting\n• the regularization term ∥\n∥λGpDp−2V ̄ − 1\nk,p2\n∥\n∥2\n2,\n• regression term ∥\n∥Ek,pZ ̄kT,pV ̄ − 1\nk,p2\n∥\n∥2\n2 and\n• accumulation term ∑N\nk=Tinit\n∥\n∥V ̄ − 1\nk,p2 Zk+1,p\n∥\n∥2\n2.\nMoreover, the theoretical analysis shows that the term ∥\n∥Ek,pZ ̄kT,pV ̄ − 1\n2 k,p\n∥\n∥2\n2 and ∑N\nk=Tinit\n∥\n∥V ̄ − 1\n2\nk,p Zk+1,p\n∥\n∥2\n2 are\ndominated by σ ̄R ̄ tr(Z ̄kT,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Z ̄k,p\n) and det(V ̃N,p) respectively. Therefore, in this subsection, to fully illustrate the essence of the effect of the forgetting factor, our analysis will mainly be based on the relationship between γ and the dominant terms.\n30\n\n\n D.1 Effect of forgetting factor γ\nFor the regularization term, we have\n∥\n∥λGpDp−2V ̄ − 1\nk,p2\n∥\n∥2\n2≤\n∑p\ni=1\nM\n( ρ(A − LC) γ\n)i−1\n.\nNote that for the special case γ = ρ(A − LC), we have\n∥\n∥λGpDp−2V ̄ − 1\nk,p2\n∥\n∥2\n2 ≤ M p ≤ M β log(N ).\nwhile for the general case when γ > ρ(A − LC), the regularization term is uniformly bounded by Mγ\nγ −ρ(A−LC )\nfor all N . Hence for all γ ≥ ρ(A − LC), the effect of γ to increase the regularization error will be bounded by O(log N ), and it will only have a sharp increase when γ is close to ρ(A − LC).\nWhile for the term ∥\n∥Ek,pZ ̄kT,pV ̄ − 1\n2 k,p\n∥\n∥2\n2, which is dominated by σ ̄R ̄tr(Z ̄kT,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Z ̄k,p\n). We have\ntr(Z ̄kT,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Z ̄k,p\n) = tr(V ̄ −1\nk,p Z ̄k,pZ ̄kT,p\n) = tr(Imp − λV ̃ −1\nk,p\n).\nFor the term tr(λV ̃ −1\nk,p\n), we have\nλI + DpZ ̄k,pZ ̄⊤\nk,pDp ≤ λI + poly\n(\nM, β, log 1\nδ\n)\nk 2κ Dp2 .\nwith high probability 1 − δ, where M is only related to system parameter. Then we have\ntr(Z ̄kT,pV ̄ −1\nk,p Z ̄k,p\n)≤\np−1\n∑\nl=0\nm − mλ\nλ+poly(M, β, log 1\nδ\n)k2κγ2l .\nThe above inequality shows that the forgetting factor γ can reduce the regression term ∥\n∥Ek,pZ ̄kT,pV ̄ − 1\n2 k,p\n∥\n∥2\n2through\nreducing poly (M, β, log 1\nδ\n) k2κγ2l. Consider the case γ = ρ(A − LC), from the selection